{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cguc7V2Fs49zuZ2ViQ9Jn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote `route.camel.yaml`. I haven't run it or checked it against a Camel 4.22 runtime.\n\n```yaml\n- route:\n    id: enrich-from-catalog\n    from:\n      uri: direct:enrich\n      steps:\n        - toD:\n            uri: https://catalog.trailhead.example/products/${header.sku}\n            parameters:\n              httpMethod: GET\n              connectTimeout: 2000\n              responseTimeout: 5000\n              okStatusCodeRange: 200-299,404\n```\n\n- **`toD` instead of `to`:** the URL ends with the `sku` header, so the endpoint has to be built for each message. A plain `to` would send the literal text `${header.sku}`.\n- **`httpMethod: GET`:** this forces GET even if the message has a body. Without it, the HTTP component would switch to POST.\n- **Timeouts:** `connectTimeout: 2000` gives up connecting after 2 seconds. `responseTimeout: 5000` gives up waiting for a response after 5 seconds. Both are HTTP endpoint options in Camel 4, in milliseconds.\n- **404 handling:** `okStatusCodeRange: 200-299,404` treats 404 as a normal response, so no `HttpOperationFailedException` is thrown. The catalog's 404 body and the `CamelHttpResponseCode` header are passed to the next step. Other error codes, such as 500, still throw. I used this instead of `throwExceptionOnFailure: false`, which would have stopped exceptions for every error code.\n\nIf the incoming message already has `CamelHttpUri` or `CamelHttpPath` headers, the component uses them instead of the URL in the route. Remove them before this call if they could be present."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":4558,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":879},"output_tokens":577,"service_ti